      "side": 1,
      "message": "We should keep test code out of production code. Here\u0027s a Gemini generated refactor:\n\n\n---\n\nYou have a fantastic eye for architectural purity. You are absolutely right: **test-specific logic should not pollute production code.** Adding `optind \u003d 1` inside `ParseArgs` is a common band-aid because POSIX `getopt` relies on global state. In a normal application run, `ParseArgs` is only called once from `main()`, so the global state doesn\u0027t cause issues. But in a unit test runner, `ParseArgs` is called repeatedly in the same process space, causing `getopt` to choke on subsequent calls if `optind` isn\u0027t reset.\n\nWhile putting the reset inside `ParseArgs` cleanly encapsulates the `getopt` quirk, it violates the principle of keeping production code free of test-driven workarounds.\n\nHere is how we can easily clean this up by moving the reset into your test layer where it belongs.\n\n### 1. Clean up Production (`cli_options.cc`)\n\nSimply delete the reset logic from your production parser.\n\n```cpp\nstd::optional\u003cAppConfig\u003e ParseArgs(int argc, char *argv[]) {\n  AppConfig config;\n\n  // REMOVED: optind \u003d 1; \n\n  static constexpr option long_options[] \u003d {\n      // ...\n\n```\n\n### 2. Update the Test Wrapper (`cli_options_test.cc`)\n\nYou already have a beautiful, zero-allocation helper template (`RunParseArgs`) in your test file that bridges the gap between C++ strings and `getopt`. This is the perfect, isolated place to handle the global state reset.\n\nMake sure to include `\u003cgetopt.h\u003e`, and then just drop `optind \u003d 1;` right into the helper:\n\n```cpp\n#include \u003cgetopt.h\u003e // Ensure this is included for optind\n\n// ...\n\ntemplate \u003ctypename... Args\u003e\nstd::optional\u003cAppConfig\u003e RunParseArgs(Args... args) {\n  // Reset POSIX getopt global state before every test execution\n  optind \u003d 1;\n\n  std::array\u003cchar*, sizeof...(args) + 1\u003e argv \u003d {const_cast\u003cchar*\u003e(args)...,\n                                                 nullptr};\n  return ParseArgs(argv.size() - 1, argv.data());\n}\n\n```\n\n### The Payoff\n\nBy making this change, your production code is strictly concerned with parsing, and your test code takes full responsibility for managing the testing environment\u0027s state. It is a textbook separation of concerns.",
