      "writtenOn": "2026-06-18T00:18:47Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "We can use `std::ranges::copy` to simplify here and other places.\n\nGemini response:\n\n----\n\nThe traditional `\u003calgorithm\u003e` approach of passing `begin()` and `end()` is prone to classic copy-paste errors (like accidentally mixing up the iterators of two different containers). The `\u003cranges\u003e` library eliminates this by allowing you to pass the container itself as a single conceptual entity.\n\nFor trivially copyable types (like `uint8_t`), the compiler will optimize both versions down to a highly efficient `memmove` or `memcpy` under the hood, so this is purely a win for safety and readability.\n\nHere are the places where you can make this upgrade across the patch:\n\n### 1. `fake_usb_comm.h` (Inside `Receive`)\n\n**Before:**\n\n```cpp\nstd::copy(response.begin(), response.end(), data.begin());\n\n```\n\n**After:**\n\n```cpp\nstd::ranges::copy(response, data.begin());\n\n```\n\n*(Note: Apply this to both instances of `std::copy` inside the `Receive` function).*\n\n### 2. `bootrom.h` (Inside `SetAesKey`)\n\n**Before:**\n\n```cpp\nvoid SetAesKey(std::span\u003cconst uint8_t, crypto::kAes256KeySize\u003e key) {\n  std::copy(key.begin(), key.end(), aes_key_.begin());\n}\n\n```\n\n**After:**\n\n```cpp\nvoid SetAesKey(std::span\u003cconst uint8_t, crypto::kAes256KeySize\u003e key) {\n  std::ranges::copy(key, aes_key_.begin());\n}\n\n```\n\n### 3. `file_util.cc` (Inside `LoadAesKey`)\n\n**Before:**\n\n```cpp\nstd::copy(key_opt.begin(), key_opt.end(), key_buffer.begin());\n\n```\n\n**After:**\n\n```cpp\nstd::ranges::copy(key_opt, key_buffer.begin());\n\n```\n\n### 4. `bootrom_test.cc` (Inside `GetFlashInfoSuccess`)\n\n**Before:**\n\n```cpp\nstd::string prod_name \u003d \"TestFlash\";\nstd::copy(prod_name.begin(), prod_name.end(), info.prod_name);\n\n```\n\n**After:**\n\n```cpp\nstd::string prod_name \u003d \"TestFlash\";\nstd::ranges::copy(prod_name, info.prod_name);\n\n```\n\n### Why does the destination still use `.begin()`?\n\nYou might wonder why we write `std::ranges::copy(response, data.begin());` instead of `std::ranges::copy(response, data);`.\n\nIn the C++ standard library, copy operations require an **output iterator**, not an output range. This is by design: it tells the compiler exactly *where* to start writing, allowing you to copy a 10-byte response into the start of a 50-byte buffer without the compiler complaining about size mismatches.\n\n*(Just ensure you have `#include \u003calgorithm\u003e` or `#include \u003cranges\u003e` wherever you make this switch!)*",
